Critically, all tokens within a response share the same advantage, as the reward signal is provided only at the sequence level. The GRPO objective incorporates a clipped surrogate loss to moderate policy updates, along with a reverse KL penalty to prevent excessive deviation from a reference policy:

\begin{split}\mathcal{L}_{\text{GRPO}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}x\sim\mathcal{S}\\ o_{1},\ldots,o_{G}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x)\end{subarray}}\Bigg[\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\frac{1}{|o_{i}|}\sum_{n=1}^{|o_{i}|}\\ \min\left(\rho_{i}^{n}A_{i},\text{clip}\left(\rho_{i}^{n},1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon\right)A_{i}\right)\\ -\beta D_{\text{KL}}[\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x)\|\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|x)]\Bigg]\end{split}

where $\rho_{i}^{n}=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i}^{n}|x,o_{i}^{<n})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(o_{i}^{n}|x,o_{i}^{<n})}$ is the importance ratio, $\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}$ is the policy before the update, and $\varepsilon$ controls the clipping range.
